   "content": "# How a Heat Pump Works\n\nA heat pump sounds like a contradiction. How can you \"pump\" heat out of the cold air outside on a winter morning? The answer is that heat is always everywhere \u2014 even in freezing air, molecules are still moving, and that movement *is* heat. A heat pump doesn't create warmth. It moves existing heat from one place to another, kind of like a conveyor belt for thermal energy.\n\n**The basic cycle**\n\nInside a heat pump, a fluid called refrigerant flows through a closed loop of pipes. This fluid has a special property: it boils at a very low temperature. That means it can turn from liquid to gas even when it's sitting in freezing air.\n\nHere's the journey, step by step:\n\n**1. Absorbing heat (the evaporator).** The refrigerant enters the outdoor coil as a cold, low-pressure liquid. Because its boiling point is so low, it quickly flashes into a gas as it sits in the outside air. To boil, it needs energy \u2014 and it pulls that energy from the surrounding air. The air loses a tiny bit of heat. The refrigerant, now a gas, is slightly warmer than it was before.\n\n**2. Squeezing it (the compressor).** A motor-driven compressor then squeezes that low-pressure gas into a smaller space. When you compress a gas, it gets hot \u2014 the same reason the back of a car tire gets warm after a long drive. The refrigerant is now a hot, high-pressure gas.\n\n**3. Releasing heat (the condenser).** This hot gas flows through the indoor coil. It's now hotter than the air inside your house, so it gives up its heat to the room. As it loses that heat, it condenses back into a liquid. Your living room gets warmer.\n\n**4. Letting it cool (the expansion valve).** The liquid then passes through a tiny valve that drops its pressure suddenly. When pressure drops, the liquid gets very cold again. It's now ready to go back outside and repeat the cycle.\n\nThat's it. The same fluid keeps looping, endlessly siphoning heat from the outdoors and dumping it indoors.\n\n**Why it works in cold weather**\n\nPeople often ask: \"If it's 10 degrees outside, where is the heat coming from?\" The answer is that 10-degree air still contains far more thermal energy than you'd expect. The heat pump doesn't need the air to be warm \u2014 it just needs the air to be warmer than the refrigerant, which it always is, because the refrigerant is engineered to be extremely cold before it hits the outdoor coil.\n\n**Flipping it for summer**\n\nThe same machine can cool your house in summer. A reversing valve simply swaps which coil is the evaporator and which is the condenser. Now the refrigerant absorbs heat from your indoor air and dumps it outside. Same cycle, opposite direction.\n\n**Why it's efficient**\n\nAn electric resistance heater converts 1 unit of electricity into 1 unit of heat. A heat pump uses that same electricity to run the compressor and fan, but it moves 3 to 5 units of heat from the outside air for every 1 unit of electricity it consumes. It's not creating heat \u2014 it's relocating it \u2014 and that's what makes it so much more efficient than a conventional furnace or baseboard heater.",
